UID:cx5-_SRhPugrvitdY3KCL
SUMMARY:INPRINT BOOK CLUB DISCUSSES HOW TO COMMIT A POSTCOLONIAL MURDER
DTSTAMP:20260810T100230Z
DTSTART:20260823T160000Z
DESCRIPTION:This Inprint Book Club meeting will take place via Zoom. The Zo
	om link will be the same for every meeting and will be provided in your Ev
	entbrite email receipt (scroll down to the Additional Information section)
	. \n \nOn August 23\, 2026 the Inprint Book Club will discuss the novel Ho
	w to Commit a Postcolonial Murder by Nina McConigley\, who will appear as 
	part of the 2025/2026 Inprint Margarett Root Brown Reading Series on Monda
	y\, May 4\, 2026. Visit brazosbookstore.com to order your copy of McConigl
	ey’s newest novel. \nInprint Book Club meetings are free and open to the p
	ublic and are facilitated by experienced book club leader Torie Ludwin. Th
	e full schedule of Inprint Book Club meetings for 2025/2026 season appears
	 on the main Inprint Book Club webpage\, which you can access by clicking 
	here. \nIf you have any further questions\, please email info@inprint.org.
	 \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-book-club-discusses-how-to
	-commit-a-postcolonial-murder/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-book-club-discusses-how-to-com
	mit-a-postcolonial-murder/
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org
 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Andrea-Bajani-and-Claire-Vaye-Watkins-closeup-
 1-for-website.jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Andrea-Bajani-and-Claire-Vaye-Watkins-closeup-1-for-website.jpg
UID:a77iMc46r5kiC2QRvpCJb
SUMMARY:Inprint Andrea Bajani & Claire Vaye Watkins Reading
DTSTAMP:20260810T100230Z
DTSTART:20260824T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:Season tickets are on sale here. \n  \nAndrea Bajani and Claire
	 Vaye Watkins will read from their new novels The Anniversary (translated 
	by Geoffrey Brock) and Yellow Pine\, respectively\, followed by an on-stag
	e conversation led by professor and National Book Critics Circle Award fin
	alist Lacy M. Johnson. The evening will conclude with a book sale and sign
	ing. The event is presented as part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett Roo
	t Brown Reading Series.  \nAndrea Bajani is one of the most recognized and
	 lauded authors of contemporary Italian and European literature. His many 
	novels include If You Kept a Record of Sins\, called “astonishing” by The 
	New York Times\, and Every Promise\, which won the Bagutta Prize\, the old
	est Italian literary prize. A self-described existential writer\, he often
	 examines the personal\, philosophical\, and political\, while confronting
	 the unanswerable. A professor and international writer-in-residence at Ri
	ce University\, Bajani joins us with his latest novel\, The Anniversary\, 
	which won the 2025 Strega Prize\, one of Europe’s major literary awards. C
	urrently being translated into more than 25 languages\, The Anniversary is
	 a subtle yet heartrending portrait of an abusive marriage in patriarchal 
	Italy and a son’s liberating decision to cut contact with his parents. The
	 Times Literary Supplement writes\, “Bajani’s prose is sharp and brims wit
	h psychological insights . . . Perhaps its most salient feature—in deft tr
	anslation by Geoffrey Brock—is its skill at depicting alienation . . . Lik
	e Albert Camus\, Bajani is a poet of estrangement.” \nClaire Vaye Watkins 
	has been called “the most riveting voice of the unsalvageable West” (Los A
	ngeles Times). Her debut novel Gold Fame Citrus\, inspired by the Californ
	ian drought and by the lives of outcasts in the desert\, blends climate fi
	ction with environmental history. Born in Bishop\, California and raised i
	n the Mojave Desert on the Amargosa River\, Watkins is also the author of 
	the novel I Love You But I’ve Chosen Darkness and the short story collecti
	on Battleborn\, winner of the Story Prize\, the Dylan Thomas Prize\, and t
	he New York Public Library’s Young Lions Fiction Award. A National Book Fo
	undation “5 Under 35” honoree and a Guggenheim Fellow\, Watkins comes to H
	ouston with her newest work Yellow Pine\, described by author Megha Majumd
	ar as “an exhilarating novel about a woman in the Mojave desert who wants 
	to be a mother one more time\, wants to love freely and arrange her life f
	reely\, wants to live outside the maze of money and ads and being sold to\
	, a novel with huge and electric convictions.” \nLacy M. Johnson is a Hous
	ton-based professor\, curator\, and activist\, and is author of The Reckon
	ings and The Other Side — both National Book Critics Circle Award finalist
	s — and Trespasses. She is editor\, with the designer Cheryl Beckett\, of 
	More City Than Water: A Houston Flood Atlas. She teaches creative nonficti
	on at Rice University\, where she is the Director of the MFA in Creative W
	riting Program\, and is also the Founding Director of the Houston Flood Mu
	seum. \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-andrea-bajani-claire-
	vaye-watkins-reading/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-andrea-bajani-claire-vaye-watk
	ins-reading/
LOCATION:Congregation Emanu El\, 1500 Sunset Boulevard\, Houston\, TX\, 770
	05
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
